so we have some actual good news out of  Israel despite the fact that it may sort  of take a while until anything really  comes of this and the news is that the  longest-serving Prime Minister Benjamin  Netanyahu has been indicted on charges  of fraud bribery and breach of trust now  as many of you know Netanyahu has been a  road block to any kind of serious peace  possibilities in Israel for a very long  time and not only the the not the only  roadblock in the circumstances that  present themselves there but a pretty  massive one particularly given his  alliances with some of the most extreme  right-wing elements in the Israeli  Knesset and he has now been indicted  this is the first prosecution in Israel  of a sitting Prime Minister and  politically things in Israel are very  hectic there have been two inconclusive  elections maybe going to a third vote  this has been going on for almost three  years with regard to possible charges  against Netanyahu and the charge is now  finally materializing now the obvious  question that most people would ask is  well what now does he leave what happens  next it's unclear this is widely  uncharted territory for now he can  remain Prime Minister if convicted he  would have to step down but it could be  three or even four years until a  possible conviction would happen and  that doesn't even account for the  possibility of appeals so this really  could be a very long time and it's going  to be more about financial but I'm sorry  not financial pressure although maybe so  give it given the level of corruption  he's allegedly involved in political  pressure it may be more about political  pressure in terms of actually making  change happen Netanyahu is apparently  planning to stay for the time being he's  immediately using very trumpian language  to describe the entire thing he's  calling it a witch hunt he's calling it  an attempted coup he wants the  investigators investigated I mean it's a  carbon carbon copy of the rhetoric that  we've seen Donald Trump employ around  investigations and allegations into his  misdeeds but then there are also some  pressing practical political questions  number one Benjamin Netanyahu is a  member of the Likud Party does the Likud  Party lose confidence in Netanyahu and  say he has to go  there are elements within the Likud  Party that are saying we need a  different leader but so far the official  position of Likud has been they're  standing by Benjamin Netanyahu what  might the quickest way B to get  Netanyahu out probably if Likud says  they're picking a different leader which  they could do at any time but again  right now it's not obvious that they're  going in that direction if Benjamin  Netanyahu stays in power it would not be  surprising that he would try to get  himself immunity through a congressional  procedure through the Israeli Knesset  but there is no Knesset right now in the  sense that Benjamin Netanyahu has not  been able to form a government since the  last election so it is a complete and  total mess what we do know what we can  say for sure is that Netanyahu has been  a disastrous prime minister from the  point of view of moving forward on peace  and some kind of even steps towards  breaking the impasse of the  israeli-palestinian conflict now every  time I talk about this if I don't  mention the both sides somebody gets mad  at me there's no question that Hamas and  Gaza has also been a disgusting  impediment to moving forward but no  matter that we still have to point out  that Benjamin Netanyahu has no  credibility as an even potential arbiter  of peace and he has to go if we want a  shot and hopefully that will be  precipitated by this although we don't  know I've said before Benjamin Netanyahu  is allied with the most extreme  religious right-wing elements of the  Israeli Knesset that is not a group that  is likely to oversee any serious  advancements in breaking the  israeli-palestinian conflict impasse  there's no credible path to a lasting  peace deal of any kind as long as  Benjamin Netanyahu is not only there but  again under indictment on these charges  Israeli politics is complex it's  unpredictable  it's not obvious what the next step will  be again a conviction on these charges  is unlikely to be the fastest way to get  him out since it could take three to  four years for an initial conviction and  then there can be appeals so this is  going to be a long one the  idiot news is of course good we have to  understand that this will be a very slow  process which might in the immediate  change very little or something could  happen likelihood saying Netanyahu's out  here's our new leader which might  actually bring change more quickly there  is probably more to gain by trying to  seat a new government then trying to  wait around for a Benjamin Netanyahu  conviction but some good news although  it's unclear how quickly it will bring  any change forward we'll follow it for  sure
 

